(Regression test: "as a float" on Text/Buffer was completely broken
before this fix - codegen called _parse_f64, which didn't exist
anywhere, causing a hard NASM assembly failure for every program using
this cast.)

A text called "s1" is "3.14".
Set f1 to s1 as a float.
Print f1.

A text called "s2" is "-2.5".
Set f2 to s2 as a float.
Print f2.

A text called "s3" is "42".
Set f3 to s3 as a float.
Print f3.

A text called "s4" is "0.001".
Set f4 to s4 as a float.
Print f4.

(Buffer input, including the exact clear+reuse pattern that caused a
real bug in integer casting elsewhere - verifying the float path uses
the same length-bounded fix from the start rather than reintroducing it)
a buffer called "buf1" is "3.14159".
Set f5 to buf1 as a float.
Print f5.

Create a buffer called buf2.
Set byte 1 of buf2 to '9'.
Set byte 2 of buf2 to '9'.
Set byte 3 of buf2 to '.'.
Set byte 4 of buf2 to '9'.
Set byte 5 of buf2 to '9'.
Set f6 to buf2 as a float.
Print f6.

clear buf2.
Set byte 1 of buf2 to '1'.
Set byte 2 of buf2 to '.'.
Set byte 3 of buf2 to '5'.
Set f7 to buf2 as a float.
Print f7.

(Degenerate inputs must not crash)
A text called "emptystr" is "".
Set f8 to emptystr as a float.
Print f8.

A text called "trailing" is "5.5abc".
Set f9 to trailing as a float.
Print f9.
